##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    3028 1451  213
                                                                  3028 1451  214
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       3028 1451  215
                                                                  3028 1451  216
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          3028 1451  217
                                                                  3028 1451  218
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   3028 1451  219
                                                                  3028 1451  220
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 3028 1451  221
                                                                  3028 1451  222
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     3028 1451  223
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   3028 1451  224
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             3028 1451  225
                                                                  3028 1451  226
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                3028 1451  227
                                                                  3028 1451  228
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       3028 1451  229
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  3028 1451  230
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   3028 1451  231
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  3028 1451  232
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  3028 1451  233
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 3028 1451  234
to the highest incident energy.                                   3028 1451  235
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:3028 1451  236
                                                                  3028 1451  237
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  3028 1451  238
                                                                  3028 1451  239
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    3028 1451  240
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     3028 1451  241
                                                                  3028 1451  242
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     3028 1451  243
                                                                  3028 1451  244
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    3028 1451  245
total cross section.                                              3028 1451  246
                                                                  3028 1451  247
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            3028 1451  248
                                                                  3028 1451  249
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    3028 1451  250
                                                                  3028 1451  251
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        3028 1451  252
                                                                  3028 1451  253
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  3028 1451  254
                                                                  3028 1451  255
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           3028 1451  256
                                                                  3028 1451  257
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   3028 1451  258
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        3028 1451  259
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     3028 1451  260
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         3028 1451  261
sections.                                                         3028 1451  262
                                                                  3028 1451  263
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 3028 1451  264
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 3028 1451  265
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 3028 1451  266
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 3028 1451  267
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      3028 1451  268
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       3028 1451  269
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              3028 1451  270
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  3028 1451  271
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  3028 1451  272
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  3028 1451  273
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  3028 1451  274
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  3028 1451  275
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       3028 1451  276
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        3028 1451  277
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        3028 1451  278
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        3028 1451  279
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        3028 1451  280
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        3028 1451  281
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        3028 1451  282
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        3028 1451  283
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       3028 1451  284
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        3028 1451  285
                                                                  3028 1451  286
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           3028 1451  287
                                                                  3028 1451  288
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    3028 1451  289
discrete levels.                                                  3028 1451  290
                                                                  3028 1451  291
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         3028 1451  292
                                                                  3028 1451  293
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  3028 1451  294
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   3028 1451  295
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      3028 1451  296
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  3028 1451  297
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      3028 1451  298
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         3028 1451  299
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   3028 1451  300
on an angular grid.                                               3028 1451  301
                                                                  3028 1451  302
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       3028 1451  303
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        3028 1451  304
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         3028 1451  305
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         3028 1451  306
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         3028 1451  307
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        3028 1451  308
                                                                  3028 1451  309
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     3028 1451  310
                                                                  3028 1451  311
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     3028 1451  312
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   3028 1451  313
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  3028 1451  314
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             3028 1451  315
All data are generated with TALYS.                                3028 1451  316
                                                                  3028 1451  317
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   3028 1451  318
                                                                  3028 1451  319
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      3028 1451  320
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   3028 1451  321
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   3028 1451  322
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are3028 1451  323
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   3028 1451  324
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  3028 1451  325
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              3028 1451  326
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 3028 1451  327
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  3028 1451  328
For each residual product:                                        3028 1451  329
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               3028 1451  330
- the recoil                                                      3028 1451  331
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         3028 1451  332
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     3028 1451  333
sections.                                                         3028 1451  334
                                                                  3028 1451  335
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     3028 1451  336
                                                                  3028 1451  337
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  3028 1451  338
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 3028 1451  339
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          3028 1451  340
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       3028 1451  341
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 3028 1451  342
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     3028 1451  343
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  3028 1451  344
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      3028 1451  345
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         3028 1451  346
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             3028 1451  347
                                                                  3028 1451  348
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   3028 1451  349
followed:                                                         3028 1451  350
                                                                  3028 1451  351
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         3028 1451  352
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         3028 1451  353
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         3028 1451  354
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     3028 1451  355
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     3028 1451  356
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     3028 1451  357
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     3028 1451  358
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     3028 1451  359
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     3028 1451  360
                                                                  3028 1451  361
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 3028 1451  362
                                                                  3028 1451  363
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    3028 1451  364
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          3028 1451  365
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     3028 1451  366
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          3028 1451  367
                                                                  3028 1451  368
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  3028 1451  369
                                                                  3028 1451  370
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     3028 1451  371
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    3028 1451  372
as isomer.                                                        3028 1451  373
                                                                  3028 1451  374
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 3028 1451  375
                                                                  3028 1451  376
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        3028 1451  377
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    3028 1451  378
as isomer.                                                        3028 1451  379
                                                                  3028 1451  380
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          3028 1451  381
                                                                  3028 1451  382
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:3028 1451  383
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   3028 1451  384
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   3028 1451  385
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   3028 1451  386
  given                                                           3028 1451  387
                                                                  3028 1451  388
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          3028 1451  389
                                                                  3028 1451  390
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  3028 1451  391
                                                                  3028 1451  392
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 3028 1451  393
                                                                  3028 1451  394
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         3028 1451  395
                                                                  3028 1451  396
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   3028 1451  397
                                                                  3028 1451  398
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        3028 1451  399
and more details are given above in this description. In most     3028 1451  400
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        3028 1451  401
                                                                  3028 1451  402
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 3028 1451  403
                                                                  3028 1451  404
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  3028 1451  405
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     3028 1451  406
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    3028 1451  407
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   3028 1451  408
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  3028 1451  409
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                3028 1451  410
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      3028 1451  411
   same reaction channel between different energies)              3028 1451  412
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      3028 1451  413
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               3028 1451  414
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      3028 1451  415
   different reaction channels for different energies)            3028 1451  416
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        3028 1451  417
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              3028 1451  418
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  3028 1451  419
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       3028 1451  420
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     3028 1451  421
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  3028 1451  422
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     3028 1451  423
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     3028 1451  424
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     3028 1451  425
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   3028 1451  426
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   3028 1451  427
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  3028 1451  428
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   3028 1451  429
                                                                  3028 1451  430
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  3028 1451  431
                                                                  3028 1451  432
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       3028 1451  433
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  3028 1451  434
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       3028 1451  435
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   3028 1451  436
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         3028 1451  437
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  3028 1451  438
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      3028 1451  439
representation is used.                                           3028 1451  440
                                                                  3028 1451  441
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      3028 1451  442
                                                                  3028 1451  443
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  3028 1451  444
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             3028 1451  445
                                                                  3028 1451  446
